doc.para:
Freestyle
　Characters generally called "whitespace", such as line breaks, spaces, and tabs, can be placed anywhere as long as the meaning does not become unclear. Also, they must be inserted appropriately so that the meaning does not become obscured.

(1)
	function func(a,b) { a++; return a+b; }

(2)
	function func(a,b) {
		a++;
		return a+b;
	}

(3)
	function func(a,b)
	{
		a++;
		return a+b;
	}

(4)
	function
	func
	(
	a
	,
	b
	)
	{
	a
	++
	;
	return
	a
	+
	b
	;
	}

(5)
	functionfunc(a,b){a++;returna+b;}

(6)
	f unction fu nc(a,b)
	{
		a+ +;

	ret urn a+b;
	}


　All of these, (1) to (4), have the exact same meaning. However, from the standpoint of readability, (2) or (3) is recommended.
　(5) is written without inserting spaces, but doing this causes the characters to run together, so it will not be interpreted correctly.
　(6) contains spaces where spaces should not be placed, so it results in an error.

Comments
　Sections enclosed by /* and */ are treated as comments and are not interpreted. Comments can be nested.
　Similarly, the portion of a line following // is also treated as a comment.


	function example(a,b,c)
	{
		return a*b/c; // Returns a*b/c
	}

	/*
		You can also write comments like this
		/* Comment nesting is allowed */
	*/
